{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794400965,"nonce":"j6qwY9baibUXnn5Mo9nYodS4QZ0=","title":"Generator: what hxn-gen covers and what it does not yet","message":"hxn-gen reads a library's source for items marked `#[hxn::interface]` and `#[hxn::methods]` and writes the manifest, the adapter over the plain C ABI, the adapter of HashLink primitives, the C header, the hxcpp glue, and the Haxe classes for both runtimes. The attributes themselves generate nothing; `hxn-macros` only makes them legal Rust and strips `#[hxn(...)]` notes.\n\nThis departs from the scope document, which describes a proc macro emitting the manifest during `cargo build`. A proc macro cannot cleanly write files, and cannot put code into the separate `-c` and `-hl` crates the document also asks for. The user approved reading the source instead on 2026-11-11.\n\nHow it is used: each thin adapter crate has a build script that calls `hxn_gen::parse` on the library's source and writes the adapter into `OUT_DIR`, which its `lib.rs` includes. The `hxn-gen` command writes the rest (manifest, headers, Haxe) into a directory.\n\nCovered, and tested by `tests/counter/run.sh gen-hl` and `gen-cpp` on Ash (hybrid, jit, interp), stock HashLink and hxcpp:\n\n- a constructor named `new`, methods taking `\u0026self` or `\u0026mut self`, and functions with no receiver, which become statics\n- parameters: bool, i32, f64, \u0026str, \u0026[u8]\n- results: (), bool, i32, f64, String, and a `Result` of any of those\n- `close()` and a finalizer on every interface\n- `#[hxn(blocking)]`\n- doc comments carried into the manifest and the Haxe classes\n- refusals with a line number for what cannot cross\n\nNot yet, in the order I would take them:\n\n1. Wasm for generated bindings: `gen-wasm` and `gen-emcc` modes. The hand-written fixture runs there; the generated one has only run natively.\n2. Futures and streams as results, lent callbacks as parameters, and `async fn`. The runtime layer has all of these; the generator emits none.\n3. Bytes as a result (`Vec\u003cu8\u003e`), which needs a length on the HashLink side.\n4. An interface as a parameter or as the result of anything but `new`; more than one source file; free functions outside an `impl`.\n5. The `hxn.Bind` build macro, which would define the classes in memory from the manifest. Today the classes are written as `.hx` files, which is the document's `hxn gen --emit-haxe`.\n6. CPPIA: the generated hxcpp class holds a raw pointer, which a script cannot.\n7. `usize`, integer widths other than i32, and the WebIDL form of the manifest.\n\nThe manifest is JSON with `\"hxn\": 1` as its version. `tests/counter/counter.hxn.json` is committed and a test fails when it no longer matches the source.","files":null}]}